      "thinking": "I'm working through the postmortem's causal chain, starting with the slow account page symptom and trying to pin down the actual latency figures before and during the incident to establish impact.\n\nThen I trace cause: io-shop's calls to io-pricing's basket-total endpoint slowed starting around 14:11, matching the incident window, with no corresponding deploy or flag change on io-shop to blame \u2014 those were ruled out since nothing changed in the day or hour prior. The deeper trigger was io-pricing itself slowing down, which a restart later resolved, though the root cause of that slowdown isn't captured in the record. I'm now reasoning that basket_total calls had no timeout set, so slowness there cascaded directly into account page load times.\n\nSo the chain is: io-pricing's basket-total calls began taking longer at onset, which account pages waited on without any timeout limit, producing the symptom. I'm checking what's ruled out \u2014 no io-shop deploys or flag changes around that window, and the baseline error rates at onset look typical, so those aren't contributing factors.\n\nNow I'm thinking about contributing factors, starting with the missing timeout on basket_total, though that's really the root cause rather than a separate contributing factor.\n\nFor other candidates: nobody being paged seems more like a standalone finding than a factor that would have shortened the incident, since Argus self-mitigated quickly anyway \u2014 I'll likely place that under \"what went wrong\" instead. Similarly, a review or testing gap that failed to catch the missing timeout before shipping could count as a contributing factor, but it risks overlapping with the root cause itself.\n\nThe stronger candidate is that io-pricing's own latency wasn't alerted on directly \u2014 detection only came via io-shop's downstream alert, meaning the issue at its source (owned by checkout-platform) went unnoticed longer than it should have. That feels like a legitimate contributing factor, with an action item to add a latency alert directly on io-pricing's basket-total endpoint.\n\nI'll leave the cause of io-pricing's slowdown unresolved as a non-precondition. Then there's the question of whether a test should have caught a slow pricing service leaving the basket total missing \u2014 tests expected a timely price response, so a missing timeout limit arguably should have been caught before shipping, though that's the familiar \"missing pre-check\" pattern the framework tends to discount.\n\nChecking further, there's no review or test gate requiring time limits on outbound dependency calls, so an action item would be adding that check to prevent recurrence \u2014 AI-2 doesn't cover this since it only merges the immediate fix. Separately, the slowdown was only surfaced via io-shop's p95 alert, not io-pricing's own latency monitoring, suggesting a gap in source-level alerting that could have caught the issue earlier.\n\nI'm also weighing whether a separate warning signal (G9feb) deserves its own corrective-action entry or should fold into the alerting gap \u2014 leaning toward skipping it as redundant. And I'm considering whether the lack of paging counts as a went-wrong item, since HighLatency alerts on io-shop aren't routed to an on-call rotation that would actually page a person.\n\nDrafting the action items now: enforce time limits on outbound dependency calls, add a latency alert specifically for io-pricing, route HighLatency alerts to on-call paging, and have checkout-platform investigate the actual root cause of io-pricing's slowdown. I'm also noting F1's proposed 300ms budget should go into the review checklist rather than prompting new test runs, and starting to list what went well, including that the investigator agent correctly placed the onset time.\n\nI'll call it a draft pull request to stay safe on naming. For what_went_wrong, nobody was paged since no on-call incident was linked to the alert, and got_lucky should stay empty since the restart actually resolved the issue rather than luck playing a role. I'm also noting the assumption linking the io-pricing slowdown to the restart, based on slow 200-response calls taking around 1500ms.\n\nFor still_at_risk, I'll phrase it as: a fresh slowdown in io-pricing could again slow every account page by the same amount, since the basket_total limit fix was never merged.\n\nFor root_cause, I'm framing it as: basket_total sets no timeout when fetching the optional basket total for the account page, so when io-pricing's calls stalled, each page waited the full duration \u2014 the fallback only triggers on PricingServiceFailed, which a slow response never raises.\n\nThen for Step 1, I'm describing how the service's p95 latency crossed the alert threshold, causing shoppers' account pages to load slowly.\n\nFor Step 2, I'm noting that from the start, io-shop's calls to io-pricing for basket totals were slow, logged as slow upstream calls, and ruling out deploys, releases, or flag changes on io-shop in the preceding day or hour as causes \u2014 pointing instead to io-pricing itself as the source, with its later restart resolving the issue.\n\nFor Step 3, each account page waits on that basket_total call with no timeout set, so a slow response gets waited out fully rather than failing fast.\n\nI'm citing F1 and U1 here, confirming the chain reaches a cause and rests properly. I should avoid mentioning replica counts or detection gaps, and need to double check phrasing so no stray digits sneak into the prose\u2014swapping out something like \"p95\" for \"slowest requests' latency\" instead.\n\n",
